WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.210 --> 00:00:08.720
paula-gearon
Hi, this is Mario Aquino.

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:11.510
I'm one of the organizers for the strange
loop conference.

00:00:11.510 --> 00:00:14.389
And this is another episode of Strange Loop
Chats.

00:00:14.389 --> 00:00:15.610
With me today is Paula Gearon.

00:00:15.610 --> 00:00:19.590
She is an engineer at Cisco in the Threat
Grid Group.

00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:23.420
And we're going to be talking today about
an open source database that Paula maintains

00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:24.680
that's called Asami.

00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:29.090
Before I get to that, I'd like to mention
that Strange Loop this year is going to, start

00:00:29.090 --> 00:00:33.750
on Thursday, September 30th, which is going
to be our workshop day.

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:38.379
And the conference proper is going to start
on Friday, October 1st and run two days the

00:00:38.379 --> 00:00:44.440
first and 2nd of October, if you'd like to
find out more you can visit https://thestrangeloop.com

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:48.260
for details about tickets and the schedule.

00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:53.620
So with that out of the way let me introduce
Paula, Paula, thank you for sitting with me

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:55.510
for this interview.

00:00:55.510 --> 00:00:56.719
Welcome.

00:00:56.719 --> 00:00:59.129
Thanks, Mario.

00:00:59.129 --> 00:01:02.899
So I wanted to talk to you today about Asami.

00:01:02.899 --> 00:01:06.450
What can you tell me about Asami?

00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:12.770
It's a graph database and it started life
as an in-memory graph database, but recently

00:01:12.770 --> 00:01:18.820
we developed it into something which also
stores persistently on disk and looking at

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:23.350
different types of provisioning for being
able to store it in lots of different ways

00:01:23.350 --> 00:01:24.759
and locations as well.

00:01:24.759 --> 00:01:30.610
But yeah, it's a graph database written entirely
in Clojure and it is there to provide ground

00:01:30.610 --> 00:01:35.149
services for Clojure developers who want to
use data in that way.

00:01:35.149 --> 00:01:40.720
So can you describe what exactly is a graph
database?

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:47.680
Graph database represents graph data where
a graph in the mathematical sense is essentially

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:48.680
like a network.

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:52.860
It looks like a full series of nodes with
lines, connecting.

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:57.350
We typically think of these things as each
node, each dot, representing some kind of

00:01:57.350 --> 00:02:03.320
resource and then the line in between will
have a label and it will be typically in a

00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:04.320
direction.

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:10.140
So if I have a resource that would represent
a person, I can have lines in which go off

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:13.900
to the different attributes of the person,
like their name, their address, and things

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:15.440
like that.

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:21.500
And I can also have lines, which connect that
person's node to nodes which represent other

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:25.710
people, either as friends or their manager
or neighbors.

00:02:25.710 --> 00:02:31.880
Then we can have knowledge which represent
companies or cities or anything at all, really.

00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:35.770
And so it's a very flexible arrangement for
data.

00:02:35.770 --> 00:02:39.910
And it provides us really consistent way of
looking at any kind of data at all.

00:02:39.910 --> 00:02:47.630
So it's a database that allows you to establish
relationships between entities that you store

00:02:47.630 --> 00:02:48.630
in it.

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:49.650
And it's written in Clojure.

00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:52.270
Is it a database that also works in ClojureScript?

00:02:52.270 --> 00:02:57.540
Originally I wrote it as Clojure and then
I was asked, can you move it up into the browsers?

00:02:57.540 --> 00:03:03.940
So we did our principal use for it at Cisco
at the moment is in the browser.

00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:08.391
And it's operating as a memory model for an
important product that we've got, which has

00:03:08.391 --> 00:03:11.000
a lot of deployment internationally now.

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:16.810
But it also works on a machine running on
the JVM and it's in that state at the moment

00:03:16.810 --> 00:03:22.980
that we've been working a lot with the persistence
model how this can be stored to disk effectively.

00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:27.670
So the name Asami can you tell me, what is
that?

00:03:27.670 --> 00:03:30.510
It's sort of an internal joke.

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:37.450
Five years ago, we started with this project,
which was the Internet Resource Orchestration

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:44.870
hub, IRA, or uncle IRA was a character in
the TV series Avatar the Last Airbender and

00:03:44.870 --> 00:03:47.370
our manager at the time really enjoyed that
show.

00:03:47.370 --> 00:03:51.430
And he started naming all these, all of these
projects after Avatar characters.

00:03:51.430 --> 00:03:55.880
That was I think IRA running in the cloud
was called Tenzin, which is from the Avatar

00:03:55.880 --> 00:03:56.880
spinoff.

00:03:56.880 --> 00:03:59.680
Well Asami is a character from Cora as well.

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:06.750
Originally I had written a rules engine, which
used Asami and that engine is called Naga

00:04:06.750 --> 00:04:10.340
again, another character from the same TV
show.

00:04:10.340 --> 00:04:15.440
So what would you say distinguishes Asami
from other graph databases?

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:20.310
Well, every graph database has its own pros
and cons.

00:04:20.310 --> 00:04:26.580
I mean, originally Asami was simply an in
memory model for Naga because we wanted something

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:29.740
which where we wouldn't need an external dependency.

00:04:29.740 --> 00:04:35.830
I was building it around Datomic and because
we were going to be doing this thing in house,

00:04:35.830 --> 00:04:40.729
I was asked well can you maybe do something
that doesn't involve a commercial system that

00:04:40.729 --> 00:04:43.780
way, this whole thing could be open source.

00:04:43.780 --> 00:04:49.150
And I, if I had thought I was going to build
an entire graph database, I'd have said no.

00:04:49.150 --> 00:04:51.650
We weren't aware of DataScript at that point.

00:04:51.650 --> 00:04:55.680
I built the basic functionality that was required
because that's all we needed.

00:04:55.680 --> 00:05:02.639
But over time as Naga needed to do more things
with rules we introduced more and more features.

00:05:02.639 --> 00:05:08.210
And finally, I realized that this storage
system had gained a life of its own and we

00:05:08.210 --> 00:05:09.949
broke it out into its own project.

00:05:09.949 --> 00:05:12.759
And now it's become dominant work for me.

00:05:12.759 --> 00:05:18.219
Where the whole query engine, the query language
everything's been expanding and you know from

00:05:18.219 --> 00:05:22.310
that point, it, then I was asked to move it
into the browser.

00:05:22.310 --> 00:05:29.150
And so it became a cross platform project
between JVM and ClojureScript in the browser.

00:05:29.150 --> 00:05:34.370
So yeah, running our own project that way
did give us the flexibility to make that move.

00:05:34.370 --> 00:05:38.810
And it's also given us the opportunity to
introduce new features as we wanted them,

00:05:38.810 --> 00:05:45.419
things like aggregates, which, you know, Datomic
already had, but also things like graph analytics.

00:05:45.419 --> 00:05:48.680
So we can do queries on transitive attributes.

00:05:48.680 --> 00:05:54.370
Or we can find sub graphs where if you've
got two distinct disconnected graphs we can

00:05:54.370 --> 00:06:00.349
identify those and, you know, different sorts
of graph algorithms like this are all things

00:06:00.349 --> 00:06:04.159
we've been able to implement internally so
that they're really optimized for the data

00:06:04.159 --> 00:06:05.159
structures.

00:06:05.159 --> 00:06:10.379
You mentioned that Asami has querying capability,
you know, you can't really have a database

00:06:10.379 --> 00:06:11.520
without a querying capability.

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:16.840
What would you say inspired the way that the
querying works in Asami?

00:06:16.840 --> 00:06:19.719
Two things.

00:06:19.719 --> 00:06:26.699
Firstly Datomic so Datomic has specified a
graph querying language, which is written

00:06:26.699 --> 00:06:30.750
in using the data structures that make up
Clojure itself.

00:06:30.750 --> 00:06:37.229
So essentially EDN, and that's been really
useful to leverage because we can read it

00:06:37.229 --> 00:06:39.629
for free, we don't have to worry about parsing.

00:06:39.629 --> 00:06:42.819
But the other thing that is based on is SPARQL.

00:06:42.819 --> 00:06:49.750
So 20 years ago I was a lead architect on
another graph database called Mulgara.

00:06:49.750 --> 00:06:52.509
And that was written entirely for the JVM.

00:06:52.509 --> 00:06:55.090
Back then we developed our own query language.

00:06:55.090 --> 00:07:02.819
And over time, it became clear that RDF data
required it's own query language standard.

00:07:02.819 --> 00:07:06.660
And so a committee was formed, which was the
SPARQL committee.

00:07:06.660 --> 00:07:08.520
I wasn't on that one.

00:07:08.520 --> 00:07:14.360
I contributed to it, but then some years later
as new features were required, the second

00:07:14.360 --> 00:07:18.789
SPARQL committee was put together and I was
on that, I was one of the editors for the

00:07:18.789 --> 00:07:19.789
documents.

00:07:19.789 --> 00:07:25.759
So there's a whole lot of semantics in SPARQL,
which differ a little bit from Datomic and

00:07:25.759 --> 00:07:30.180
because of some of the architectural decisions
that we've made in Asami it made much more

00:07:30.180 --> 00:07:37.080
sense to go often with the SPARQL semantics
over the the Datomic semantics.

00:07:37.080 --> 00:07:46.979
So the syntax is some kind of a, a union between
Datomic query language and SPARQL semantics.

00:07:46.979 --> 00:07:48.289
Mostly in the edge cases.

00:07:48.289 --> 00:07:49.550
In most queries.

00:07:49.550 --> 00:07:54.351
You're going to find that you can copy paste
something from a Datomic or DataScript, and

00:07:54.351 --> 00:07:57.009
just use it on Asami and it'll just work.

00:07:57.009 --> 00:08:03.389
But there are a few, a few things where you're
going to find like doing a, NOT operation

00:08:03.389 --> 00:08:11.319
or certain kinds of unions where the edge
cases lean more towards SPARQL, interpretation

00:08:11.319 --> 00:08:14.050
of the world, rather than Datomic.

00:08:14.050 --> 00:08:20.729
So what would you say is sort of a, an ideal
use case for for Asami where, where does it

00:08:20.729 --> 00:08:22.770
really shine?

00:08:22.770 --> 00:08:25.159
When you've got a lot of disparate data.

00:08:25.159 --> 00:08:29.080
So a lot of data that getting from different
places where you want to find linkages between

00:08:29.080 --> 00:08:35.780
the data where you want to take data which
may have come from tables, but when you put

00:08:35.780 --> 00:08:41.210
it together, you'd otherwise require dozens
and dozens of tables with lots of foreign

00:08:41.210 --> 00:08:44.690
joins and things like that if it were in the
relational world.

00:08:44.690 --> 00:08:48.770
When you're bringing a lot of data together
like that, then graph databases in general

00:08:48.770 --> 00:08:50.510
are very useful that way.

00:08:50.510 --> 00:08:56.840
Asami has been designed internally to be optimized
around querying.

00:08:56.840 --> 00:09:01.870
It's optimized around querying, but you know,
at the expense of taking a little longer to

00:09:01.870 --> 00:09:03.260
load data up.

00:09:03.260 --> 00:09:08.840
So if you're going to be doing, as someone
mentioned yesterday 30 transactions a second,

00:09:08.840 --> 00:09:10.870
then the timing is going to be slow on it.

00:09:10.870 --> 00:09:17.160
But if you're only, you know, if you're loading
your data up once or every few minutes then,

00:09:17.160 --> 00:09:20.890
and querying it a lot and analyzing it a lot,
then Asami's really going to shine.

00:09:20.890 --> 00:09:25.120
And, you know, Asami can still handle a lot
of transactions, but there are other systems

00:09:25.120 --> 00:09:29.500
which are going to be optimized more around
them, and yet they won't necessarily answer

00:09:29.500 --> 00:09:30.600
queries as quickly.

00:09:30.600 --> 00:09:36.620
You're actively maintaining Asami what is
it that you're sort of focusing on right now

00:09:36.620 --> 00:09:39.030
in terms of the feature set that you're trying
to bring to it?

00:09:39.030 --> 00:09:44.970
I'm focusing on what my colleagues at work
say they need.

00:09:44.970 --> 00:09:49.940
So when someone says like, can I do this or
doesn't that, does this work the way I think

00:09:49.940 --> 00:09:53.590
it should then that's my main priority.

00:09:53.590 --> 00:09:57.290
But when people aren't asking me for that
I've been working on storage.

00:09:57.290 --> 00:10:04.250
We're just coming up to a 2.0 release, which
is where the, the durable storage the not

00:10:04.250 --> 00:10:07.480
entirely confined to memory anymore is finally
going to be released.

00:10:07.480 --> 00:10:09.510
There's more architecture to happen there.

00:10:09.510 --> 00:10:14.290
There are certain elements of it which I'm
really happy with and other things which are

00:10:14.290 --> 00:10:17.580
known, could use more effective data structures.

00:10:17.580 --> 00:10:19.810
But it works really well at the moment.

00:10:19.810 --> 00:10:22.370
And I'm looking forward to just getting that
out the door.

00:10:22.370 --> 00:10:28.000
And then we've got more more layers of indexing
and and features that we want to have there.

00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:31.530
You know there's tradeoffs at every level.

00:10:31.530 --> 00:10:37.950
But we're looking at, at introducing some
of these new features to make the requirements

00:10:37.950 --> 00:10:43.390
that my colleagues have, frankly to, you know,
to speed up certain things and to make data

00:10:43.390 --> 00:10:50.300
available easily in, in some ways where it
may not be so easy to get to it at the moment.

00:10:50.300 --> 00:10:57.650
So in terms of durable storage, what is the
sort of goal for being able to, to persist

00:10:57.650 --> 00:10:58.750
the database?

00:10:58.750 --> 00:11:04.930
I guess, in, in it's in the, in, in a format
that has already some, some amount of, of

00:11:04.930 --> 00:11:07.640
entity data in it.

00:11:07.640 --> 00:11:08.970
Is it for replication?

00:11:08.970 --> 00:11:13.950
Is it for quick loading, like a database that
already contains ?

00:11:13.950 --> 00:11:19.850
So everything's immutable internally and I
have a whole lot of documentation around this,

00:11:19.850 --> 00:11:21.580
on the on the project website.

00:11:21.580 --> 00:11:27.440
And it, you know, internally the Clojure itself
uses these kinds of data structures.

00:11:27.440 --> 00:11:33.140
The last database I was working on Mulgara
it also used that kind of data structure.

00:11:33.140 --> 00:11:37.140
Because we have a whole lot of immutable data
structures, so things which never change,

00:11:37.140 --> 00:11:43.060
we only add to the storage, this means that
replication is really quite easy.

00:11:43.060 --> 00:11:46.060
We can just take things, knowing that they're
never going to change again.

00:11:46.060 --> 00:11:51.800
We can then spread it across a cluster because
again, we know that once something's.

00:11:51.800 --> 00:11:54.540
been written, it never changes.

00:11:54.540 --> 00:11:58.390
And this gives us a whole lot of advantages
almost for free.

00:11:58.390 --> 00:12:04.200
One of the one of the things which, you know,
when Datomic came out, it seemed like a revelation

00:12:04.200 --> 00:12:09.910
was that you could go back in time and look
at how things used to be.

00:12:09.910 --> 00:12:14.730
Ironically, Mulgara always had that kind of
data structure, but we built it 20 years ago

00:12:14.730 --> 00:12:17.060
and disk wasn't as cheap as it is now.

00:12:17.060 --> 00:12:22.180
And consequently, we needed to clean up any
data that was old that we weren't using anymore.

00:12:22.180 --> 00:12:27.110
And, you know, for, for performance reasons
over time, I eventually ripped that, that

00:12:27.110 --> 00:12:32.850
code out because it all, that bookkeeping
and cleanliness was you know, had a cost to

00:12:32.850 --> 00:12:33.850
the database.

00:12:33.850 --> 00:12:36.050
And so I was just leaving these things hanging
around.

00:12:36.050 --> 00:12:41.820
And then when Datomic came out and we were
showing that we had this rich history of what

00:12:41.820 --> 00:12:44.010
all the data looked like getting to this point.

00:12:44.010 --> 00:12:48.860
I realized at that point that Mulgara already
had that, but it just hadn't exposed it to

00:12:48.860 --> 00:12:49.860
the user.

00:12:49.860 --> 00:12:53.320
Asami has that now and it's been inspired
by Mulgara structures.

00:12:53.320 --> 00:12:54.640
I modernized them.

00:12:54.640 --> 00:12:59.810
I updated things over the years as I came
up with better ideas as to how these things

00:12:59.810 --> 00:13:00.810
should be built.

00:13:00.810 --> 00:13:06.820
And yes, a number of the features, which which
Datomic has, which make it like very different

00:13:06.820 --> 00:13:10.760
to other sorts of databases before it uh,
are also present in Asami.

00:13:10.760 --> 00:13:16.890
You know, I kind of, while I'm not aiming
to do exactly the same sorts of things, as

00:13:16.890 --> 00:13:24.140
Datomic at the same time, it has led me to
certain features thinking, you know, I kinda

00:13:24.140 --> 00:13:25.140
like that.

00:13:25.140 --> 00:13:28.550
I'd like to have my, have that in my system
as well.

00:13:28.550 --> 00:13:31.340
And sure enough people, you know, for the
things I haven't finished doing people come

00:13:31.340 --> 00:13:33.890
to me and say, when do we get this feature?

00:13:33.890 --> 00:13:35.320
So I know that it's wanted.

00:13:35.320 --> 00:13:42.940
Community-wise there are I guess groups outside
of Cisco that are either using or experimenting

00:13:42.940 --> 00:13:44.100
with Asami?

00:13:44.100 --> 00:13:50.911
So a friend of mine started a channel on the
Clojurians slack and a number of people have

00:13:50.911 --> 00:13:51.911
been joining it.

00:13:51.911 --> 00:13:56.140
I've seen people saying that they're assessing
Asami for their workplace.

00:13:56.140 --> 00:13:57.290
I've had feedback.

00:13:57.290 --> 00:14:02.590
I've had pull requests to, you know, to create
new features or or to expand on things.

00:14:02.590 --> 00:14:08.760
And so there seems to be a lot of interest
about what I'm doing, which is really quite

00:14:08.760 --> 00:14:09.760
nice.

00:14:09.760 --> 00:14:13.210
And you know, I, I want to be responsive to
the people who are using it as well.

00:14:13.210 --> 00:14:14.210
Yeah.

00:14:14.210 --> 00:14:19.370
I mean, this was kind of a hobby of mine that
I was doing on the side and I showed my manager

00:14:19.370 --> 00:14:22.150
years ago and he said, this looks great, do
it for work.

00:14:22.150 --> 00:14:27.990
And you know, I'm in a really privileged position
that I get to do something, which I was finding

00:14:27.990 --> 00:14:30.460
interesting for my job.

00:14:30.460 --> 00:14:36.550
And, you know, I want to be as responsive
to people as I can be so that I get to keep

00:14:36.550 --> 00:14:37.550
doing this job.

00:14:37.550 --> 00:14:42.660
So for folks interested in checking out Asami
for themselves, where should they go?

00:14:42.660 --> 00:14:44.030
To GitHub.

00:14:44.030 --> 00:14:45.840
It's under threadgrid/asami.

00:14:45.840 --> 00:14:48.970
It's an open-source project, all the documentation's
online.

00:14:48.970 --> 00:14:52.801
I do work hard to try to make sure that everything
is really well-documented.

00:14:52.801 --> 00:14:57.540
A lot of times people ask me for how things
are working and I'm able to point them at

00:14:57.540 --> 00:15:01.260
docs and that's really paid for itself.

00:15:01.260 --> 00:15:05.210
So hopefully if you need to find things, then
you can go there.

00:15:05.210 --> 00:15:10.090
Otherwise you can always go to the Clojurians
slack and look for #asami.

00:15:10.090 --> 00:15:14.760
If you ask questions, I do try to be as responsive
as I can be.

00:15:14.760 --> 00:15:15.920
All right.

00:15:15.920 --> 00:15:23.190
Well, Paula, I want to thank you for sitting
down for this Strange Loop chat and for folks

00:15:23.190 --> 00:15:28.940
who are watching, if you would like to find
out more about Asami there will be links when

00:15:28.940 --> 00:15:33.100
this video is posted to where you can read
about it on GitHub.

00:15:33.100 --> 00:15:34.570
Well, Paula, thanks again.

00:15:34.570 --> 00:15:39.600
It's been great talking to you.

00:15:39.600 --> 00:15:43.880
You're welcome.

00:15:43.880 --> 00:15:48.160
Thanks Mario.

